or the British Medical Association is  balloting Junior doctors now over a  future strike it's chair Dr Philip  Banfield is with us now thanks so much  for being with us good morning Sophie um  first of all can you just give us a bit  of an honest appraisal about what it's  actually like to work with the NHS right  now well our members have have never  seen so much uh stress so much moral  injury from not being able to undertake  the care that they're so desperate to  give  um this has arisen because we just don't  have enough beds for the Health Service  need at this point in time now part of  that is not being able to discharge  people from hospital and that creates a  a kind of Log Jam but this is years and  years of running down the number of beds  so we've got half the number of beds  available to the Health Service that we  did 30 years ago you talk about years  and years I think that is quite  important isn't it because  um the government often talks a lot  about covered and says that this is a  part of the knock-on effect of the  pandemic is that right what the pandemic  has done is is aggravate a situation  that was deteriorating and we have been  highlighting really for 10 years or more  so you know if you look at the figures  the waiting list had gone from 2.6 to  4.4 million before the pandemic you know  it's gone over 7 million now this is an  absolute National scandal  we spoke a program last week to at the  chair of the sort of acute emergency  medicine and he was talking about in  fact this basically means people dying  and if an ambulance can't get to them in  time if they're waiting for a e you know  is that is that the brutal reality then  uh that's a really good word even he's  brutal on the front line uh at the  moment we see our GP colleagues you know  buckling under the weight not only of of  people who are worried about things that  are going on now but they're bearing the  the brunt of the backlog because you  know people keep going to their their GP  and what does that mean does that mean  like you know hundreds of people trying  to call eight in the morning to try and  get an appointment and failing to well  well it does it means that they're  filling the face-to-face slots and  having 30 40 50 online consultations as  well to try and fit in but what it  translates to is  you know patients making conscious  decisions with their GPS not to go to  hospital when they when they need to  because they don't want to languish on a  a hospital uh trolley and my wife who is  a is a GP had 12 hours on a on an a e  trolley with uh chest pain thinking that  she was going to to die on her own  because I wasn't around at the time and  you know this is the kind of trauma that  is being suffered by patients and the  staff could not be trying harder you  know there's no fault on the staff how  on Earth emergency departments staff  keep going and we do know that it's  affecting their mental health and we do  know that it's causing burnout I'm  really sorry to hear about your wife  that's like incredibly sort of stressful  situation for her and also for you she's  better now that's going to work so I'm  glad but it does I guess highlight what  so many people are experiencing and  we've seen nurses and ambulance workers  going on strike at Doctor strikes  inevitable all uh they're not inevitable  at all we're balloting at the moment and  the result will come in five weeks time  and now is the time for us to get down  with government and vice versa to find a  resolution to this now we met with the  Secretary of State for the first time  but actually now that we've done that it  was uh not as negative as I was  expecting  um there are things that the Secretary  of State can do business with us over  and and that would include trying to  give back confidence in the pay review  bodies for example but there are things  as he points out that it's the Prime  Minister and chancellor who are  controlling  um and uh he made the same offer to us  as he's made to the other unions which  is try and help him with the economic  argument and the economic argument is  strong we're spending a lot of money on  locums with the the fact that the  the health and wealth of the population  is deteriorating and that gap between  the poor and the rich is widening and  that translates into you know an  economic crisis as well and you know it  doesn't make sense uh to make people  sicker and we know that the country is  getting sicker as a whole  let's get people back into work then  they can pay their taxes so uh you know  I for me it's a very straightforward uh  Market uh economics argument uh doctors  are leaving the NHS they've got choice  these are bright young graduates  um you know their entry pay just over 14  pounds an hour you know full pay  restoration for them means 18 pounds an  hour you know we have to decide as a  country what the value for the expertise  for junior doctors is and Junior doctor  us are not Junior by any means these are  skilled highly trained professionals and  I'm interested quite heartened I guess  to say that you think that it perhaps  wasn't quite as negative as you would  expect when you met with a health  secretary  um Steve Barkley and do you think that  he's willing to move on pay if you get  back a bit on reform that was that  effectively what happened well I've met  him for just over an hour right that's  the first time that that I've met  um so you know it is all for what  happens next  um and the indications are that we will  be having that dialogue while we're  having dialogue there's always the  possibility of averting industrial  action no doctor wants to go on strike  no nurse wants to go on strike we feel  that we're being backed into it  um I'm interested to get your thoughts  on Labor's position as well uh kissed  armor in an interview today with the  Sunday Telegraph so the NHS must reform  will die and the idea that the service  is still the Envy of the world is wrong  is he right  well when the NHS works it is superb is  it worth right now you cannot beat it  and they're a pocket there are times  when it works it's not working for  everyone  right so there are some really really  brilliant bits of the NHS that happen  day in and day out and we are  emphasizing that the NHS is the future  for this country because it should be  the most effective and efficient Health  Service you can have because if you  don't take profit out of it that money  should be available  but I mean labor says that they would  like to for example uh bypass GPS allow  people to have safe self-refer is that  is that a good idea I met with  registrating this uh week and we had to  agree to disagree that he doesn't  understand general practice  um and you know the GPS are one of the  most efficient and effective parts of  the Health Service in the way that they  manage risk they are superb at it so you  don't think that labor really  understands how the GP system works well  I think that they have a lot of work to  do between now and an election we have  offered to help them with that we'll do  business with anyone who has the right  policies this is not about Party  politics for us as a BMI so why is  self-refer a bad idea because some  people think look it's obvious you know  I can't get through to my GP um I can  just self-refer way more efficient  um it's not efficient from the point of  view that uh what tends to happen is you  have people who are the worried well and  the people who really have an issue say  I don't want to bother my doctor right I  don't want to have  um uh take up someone else's time so you  then end up  paradoxically with a widening Health  inequality because some people will just  refer themselves straight to  um Specialists when actually they don't  need to be correct okay understood  um I mean I guess what West rooting  would say is that you may be part of the  problem he's described the BMA as  hostile to his plans he says he's not  afraid to take on the doctors and  Kirsten as well telling me last weekend  we've got to have battles my experience  of Reform is there will always be people  who say I wouldn't do that I disregard  that we're not going to back down the  first time someone says I wouldn't back  down or I wouldn't do this  they're willing to go into battle with  you doctors are not the problem they are  the solution  you need the us as experts to deal with  the problems of the NHS we are not  averse to change it's a complete fallacy  that doctors want in a Health Service to  stay as it is we're the first people to  say we want change in the Health Service  but please can we have it evidence-based  please can you listen to Frontline staff  and please can you invest properly in a  Health Service we can be proud of uh  really interesting to talk thanks so  much to be uh on the for being on the  program thank you so much thank you

